A hird task has become claimable and one of several agents should take it.
---
# The routing page. Copy it to this plugin's config directory as route.jev
# (the wire pane prints the path) and the relay starts asking it which
# harness gets first refusal on each announcement. Delete it and the relay
# goes back to walking the roster in the order you wrote.
#
# Four things this file has to get right:
#
#   - The question must be named `harness`. That is the answer route.sh
#     reads; a page without it is an opinion the relay cannot hear.
#   - Every label must be a harness name hird itself uses — what `hird
#     agents` prints, and what the third column of dispatch.conf carries —
#     because that column is what the answer is matched against. A label of
#     `claude` where the roster says `claude-code` routes nothing, and is cut
#     from the question rather than asked about; the startup report names it.
#   - Write every harness you run, not only the ones you expect to win. The
#     relay cuts this list down to the harnesses each task may actually go to
#     — recusal and required capabilities are applied to the labels, not only
#     to the answer — so a page that lists two agents has nothing left to say
#     about a review recused from one of them.
#   - The descriptions are the whole rubric. They are what the model reads
#     about your agents, so write what each one is actually good at here,
#     not what it is called.
#
# The state above the rule is a placeholder: the relay replaces it with the
# task, so what is written there only matters when you are shaping the page
# by hand (`jev run route.jev --state "..."`, or `jev` with `:open`).
#
# Worth doing before trusting it: label thirty tasks you have already routed
# by hand and run `jev eval route.jev --cases routed.jsonl`. The confidence
# table says what HIRD_JEV_CONFIDENCE buys you, which beats guessing at 0.6.

harness: Which harness is the better tool for this particular task
  claude-code = Ambiguous briefs, architectural decisions, sprawling multi-file changes, work whose shape is not yet known
  codex = A clear spec carried out exactly: mechanical edits, test writing, a rename across a package, a single-file change
  copilot = Small local fixes in code somebody already has open
